WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.285
"Cancer from Benzene from the Preservative
Sodium Benzoate in Beverages"

00:00:07.252 --> 00:00:08.720
Benzene,

00:00:08.720 --> 00:00:11.920
a carcinogen that
can cause cancer in humans,

00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:15.520
can form in beverages
containing benzoate preservatives

00:00:15.520 --> 00:00:19.040
and certain acids
for example, Fanta soda.

00:00:19.040 --> 00:00:22.000
Interesting that
Coca Cola chose to disclose

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:25.520
that the artificial colors
it uses can cause ADHD symptoms

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:28.720
rather than
just not using them.

00:00:28.720 --> 00:00:30.960
Anyway, the U.S.
Food and Drug Administration

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:32.720
was informed
by the soda industry

00:00:32.720 --> 00:00:34.560
about the hazard back in 1990,

00:00:34.560 --> 00:00:38.640
yet, 15 years later, was still
finding it in some soft drinks.

00:00:38.640 --> 00:00:42.240
This prompted a national survey
of nearly 200 beverages

00:00:42.240 --> 00:00:45.440
for the presence of benzene
in soda and other beverages.

00:00:45.440 --> 00:00:49.280
The maximum contaminant level
of benzene considered safe

00:00:49.280 --> 00:00:51.280
by the U.S Environmental
Protection Agency

00:00:51.280 --> 00:00:53.600
is zero, in water supplies,

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:56.160
though that's not always
possible to reach

00:00:56.160 --> 00:00:58.400
since benzene
spews from cigarettes,

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:00.400
car exhaust,
and fireplaces.

00:01:00.400 --> 00:01:03.600
So, the European Union
allows one part per billion,

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:06.880
and the United States ended up
allowing five parts per billion

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:10.320
since it would be too costly
to push for less cancer.

00:01:10.320 --> 00:01:12.960
California holds the line
at one part per billion,

00:01:12.960 --> 00:01:17.360
but its goal for public health
is 0.15 parts per billion

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:19.680
based on cancer risk
from leukemia

00:01:19.680 --> 00:01:22.160
because a lifetime exposure
to one part per billion

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:25.040
would cause seven additional
people per million to get cancer

00:01:25.040 --> 00:01:26.560
who otherwise wouldn't.

00:01:26.560 --> 00:01:29.600
Okay, so,
how did the beverages do?

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:31.680
Of the 199 beverages tested,

00:01:31.680 --> 00:01:34.400
37 percent had no
detectable benzene,

00:01:34.400 --> 00:01:37.040
and another third had less
than one part per billion,

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:41.360
but 40 beverages exceeded the
European and California water standards,

00:01:41.360 --> 00:01:45.440
and 18 violated
U.S. federal standards.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:48.080
However, soda is
not the only source.

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:50.720
In a survey
of 451 beverage samples,

00:01:50.720 --> 00:01:53.840
benzene was found
as a heat-induced contaminant

00:01:53.840 --> 00:01:56.160
of carrot juice for babies.

00:01:56.160 --> 00:01:58.960
And 94 percent of the
carrot juice for infants

00:01:58.960 --> 00:02:01.760
had detectable levels
of this carcinogen

00:02:01.760 --> 00:02:04.880
with average concentrations
around two parts per billion,

00:02:04.880 --> 00:02:09.600
but no benzene was found in
unheated fresh carrot juice.

00:02:09.600 --> 00:02:12.720
Obviously, water standards
are much stricter,

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:15.120
but if the only
safe intake is zero,

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.160
any avoidable exposure to
benzene should be avoided.

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:22.900
And tainted soft drinks
are definitely avoidable.

